# kiosk-linux/configs/desktop.fragment
# RMPG Flex Desktop (sub-project 6) — turns the single-purpose kiosk into a
# Windows-like general-purpose workstation: taskbar with Start menu, real
# draggable/resizable windows, a file manager with desktop icons, a full
# tabbed web browser, and a terminal.
#
# Appended to configs/qemu_x86_64_kiosk_defconfig via `make defconfig` reading
# both files (see build.sh). Every symbol below was verified against the real
# pinned Buildroot 2024.02.9 checkout rather than guessed — this project has
# been burned repeatedly by symbols that silently do not exist and drop out of
# .config with no error.
#
# WHY X11 AND NOT WAYLAND: the existing kiosk browser (cog --platform=drm)
# talks straight to DRM/KMS with no compositor, which is exactly why it can
# only ever show one fullscreen page. A desktop needs a window manager, and
# X.org + Openbox is the shortest path to real Windows-style window
# decorations, minimize/maximize/close, alt-tab, and edge snapping — all of
# which Openbox provides natively. The X.org "modesetting" driver is built
# into the server (no separate package since xserver 1.18) and drives BOTH
# QEMU's virtio-gpu and the FZ-55's Intel i915 through the same KMS interface
# the kiosk build already proved works.

# ── X.org server ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
BR2_PACKAGE_XORG7=y
BR2_PACKAGE_XSERVER_XORG_SERVER=y
BR2_PACKAGE_XSERVER_XORG_SERVER_MODULAR=y
# libinput input driver: keyboard, mouse/touchpad, and the FZ-55's touchscreen.
BR2_PACKAGE_XDRIVER_XF86_INPUT_LIBINPUT=y
# startx / xinit — how the session is launched from the init script.
BR2_PACKAGE_XAPP_XINIT=y
# Display geometry query + root-window setup used by the session script.
BR2_PACKAGE_XAPP_XRANDR=y
BR2_PACKAGE_XAPP_XSETROOT=y
BR2_PACKAGE_XAPP_XDPYINFO=y

# ── Window manager ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Openbox: stacking WM with real title bars, window buttons, alt-tab, and
# keyboard-driven half-screen snapping. Themed to a Windows-like look via the
# rootfs overlay's openbox rc.xml/themerc.
BR2_PACKAGE_OPENBOX=y

# ── GTK3 stack (shell, file manager, browser all use it) ─────────────────────
# GTK3's X11 GDK backend has a hard `depends on BR2_PACKAGE_HAS_LIBGL` — full
# desktop OpenGL, not the GLES/EGL the kiosk build already had. Only
# BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_OPENGL_GLX provides that symbol (mesa3d/Config.in:345).
# Without it BR2_PACKAGE_LIBGTK3_X11 silently drops out of .config, which then
# takes rmpg-shell with it — caught for real by the symbol-verification guard
# in build.sh rather than surfacing as a missing taskbar an hour later.
BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_OPENGL_GLX=y
BR2_PACKAGE_LIBGTK3=y
BR2_PACKAGE_LIBGTK3_X11=y
BR2_PACKAGE_ADWAITA_ICON_THEME=y
BR2_PACKAGE_HICOLOR_ICON_THEME=y
BR2_PACKAGE_SHARED_MIME_INFO=y

# ── Desktop applications ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# pcmanfm doubles as the file manager AND the desktop-icon/wallpaper layer
# (--desktop mode), which is why no separate desktop-shell package is needed.
BR2_PACKAGE_PCMANFM=y
# WebKitGTK is the browser ENGINE; the browser itself is rmpg-browser, built
# from kiosk-linux/rmpg-browser in this repo.
#
# Midori was the obvious off-the-shelf choice and was tried first — it fails.
# Midori depends on libpeas, which forces BR2_PACKAGE_GOBJECT_INTROSPECTION on
# globally, and that broke the build for real: pango could not generate
# Pango-1.0.gir because harfbuzz had already been built (for wpewebkit) WITHOUT
# introspection, so HarfBuzz-0.0.gir did not exist. Recovering means force-
# rebuilding the whole glib/harfbuzz/cairo/pango gir chain. WebKitGTK itself
# only builds gir when introspection is already on (webkitgtk.mk:71 guards it),
# so writing our own browser against the engine sidesteps the entire problem —
# and drops granite, libpeas, gcr and python3 from the image as a bonus.
BR2_PACKAGE_WEBKITGTK=y
# Terminal — admin/diagnostic use; hidden from the Start menu in locked-down
# mode (see the rmpg-shell menu config in the rootfs overlay).
BR2_PACKAGE_XTERM=y

# ── First-party desktop components ───────────────────────────────────────────
# rmpg-shell (taskbar, Start menu, status indicators, Wi-Fi picker) and
# rmpg-browser (tabbed WebKitGTK browser). Source lives in this repo under
# kiosk-linux/rmpg-shell; build.sh copies the package definition into the
# Buildroot tree and registers it in package/Config.in.
BR2_PACKAGE_RMPG_SHELL=y

# ── Bundled applications ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A workstation needs more than a browser. All verified present in the pinned
# Buildroot 2024.02.9 checkout.
BR2_PACKAGE_MUPDF=y        # PDF viewer (X11 GUI) — warrants, reports, statutes
BR2_PACKAGE_FEH=y          # Image viewer — field photos, evidence stills
BR2_PACKAGE_HTOP=y         # Task manager (runs in a terminal window)
BR2_PACKAGE_NANO=y         # Console text editor, admin use
BR2_PACKAGE_VIM=y          # Console text editor, admin use
BR2_PACKAGE_MC=y           # Midnight Commander — console file manager, admin use

# ── Wi-Fi ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Promoted from out-of-scope to REQUIRED on 2026-07-24: the deployment sites
# have no wired ethernet drop, so Wi-Fi is the only path to rmpgutah.us.
# Kernel side lives in configs/kernel-wifi.fragment.
#
# connman is the network manager rather than raw wpa_supplicant + udhcpc
# scripting: it owns scanning, association, DHCP, and — critically for an
# unattended terminal — it PERSISTS known networks and reconnects on its own
# after a reboot or a dropout. connmanctl gives the desktop Wi-Fi picker a
# stable CLI to drive, with no D-Bus client code to maintain.
BR2_PACKAGE_CONNMAN=y
BR2_PACKAGE_CONNMAN_WIFI=y
BR2_PACKAGE_CONNMAN_CLIENT=y
BR2_PACKAGE_WPA_SUPPLICANT=y
BR2_PACKAGE_WPA_SUPPLICANT_NL80211=y
# EAP: law-enforcement and municipal networks are frequently WPA2-Enterprise,
# which plain WPA2-PSK support cannot join at all.
BR2_PACKAGE_WPA_SUPPLICANT_EAP=y
BR2_PACKAGE_WPA_SUPPLICANT_CLI=y
BR2_PACKAGE_IW=y

# Regulatory database — without it the card uses the most restrictive world
# domain and may not see 5 GHz networks.
BR2_PACKAGE_WIRELESS_REGDB=y

# Intel Wi-Fi firmware. iwlwifi is inert without the matching blob. The FZ-55
# ships different radios across revisions, so cover the plausible range rather
# than betting on one: 9xxx (Wireless-AC 9560, Mk1), 6E (Wi-Fi 6 AX200/AX201,
# later units), QuZ (AX201 in some SKUs), and 8265 for older stock.
BR2_PACKAGE_LINUX_FIRMWARE=y
BR2_PACKAGE_LINUX_FIRMWARE_IWLWIFI_9XXX=y
BR2_PACKAGE_LINUX_FIRMWARE_IWLWIFI_6E=y
BR2_PACKAGE_LINUX_FIRMWARE_IWLWIFI_QUZ=y
BR2_PACKAGE_LINUX_FIRMWARE_IWLWIFI_8265=y

# ── Intel graphics firmware (DMC) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# (2026-07-25) i915 does basic modesetting with no firmware, which is why
# bring-up never needed this. What it CANNOT do without DMC (the display
# microcontroller blob) is enter display power states: runtime suspend of the
# display engine is explicitly gated on a working DMC in i915's own
# intel_dmc.c, so a unit without it runs the display engine at full power for
# the whole shift. On a battery-powered terminal in a hot cabin that shows up
# as both shorter runtime and more heat — the two things this pass is about.
#
# Symbol name verified against the pinned Buildroot 2024.02.9 checkout
# (package/linux-firmware/Config.in:27 — BR2_PACKAGE_LINUX_FIRMWARE_I915; note
# it is NOT under the INTEL_* naming the neighbouring ethernet entries use).
#
# ⚠️ This package installs the WHOLE i915 firmware directory: 26.9 MB, of which
# 25 MB is GuC/HuC for GPU command submission and media offload that Gen9.5
# does not use by default and this image never enables. All 30 DMC blobs
# together are 888 KB. scripts/prune-firmware.sh therefore keeps every *_dmc*
# blob and drops GuC/HuC — generation-proof (no guessing which FZ-55 revision
# is in front of you) and it keeps 25 MB off a rootfs whose OTA payload is
# already too large to upload in one PUT.
BR2_PACKAGE_LINUX_FIRMWARE_I915=y

# ── Power management (battery indicator) ─────────────────────────────────────
# acpid delivers lid/power-button events. The taskbar battery gauge itself
# reads /sys/class/power_supply directly rather than going through upower —
# one fewer daemon, no D-Bus dependency, and it works identically on the
# FZ-55's dual batteries (BAT0 + BAT1) as on a machine with none (QEMU
# reports no power supply at all, and the indicator simply hides).
BR2_PACKAGE_ACPID=y

# ── Fonts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Liberation is metric-compatible with Arial/Times/Courier, so pages and
# documents authored against the Microsoft core fonts lay out correctly — the
# closest legal match to a Windows font stack. DejaVu (already enabled in the
# base defconfig for WebKit) stays as the fallback.
BR2_PACKAGE_LIBERATION=y

# ── Idle lock ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# libXScrnSaver provides the X Screen Saver extension, which is the only
# reliable way to ask X "how long since the user last touched this machine"
# across keyboard, mouse AND touchscreen. Polling input devices directly would
# miss the FZ-55 touchscreen, and a timer in the shell cannot see input that
# went to another window.
#
# Needed because a patrol terminal left unattended sits there displaying live
# dispatch, warrant and person data to anyone who walks past the vehicle.
BR2_PACKAGE_XLIB_LIBXSCRNSAVER=y

# ── FZ-55 hardware enablement, userspace side (2026-07-25 hardware audit) ────
# Kernel-side counterparts live in configs/kernel-{audio,bluetooth,fz55}.fragment.
# Every symbol below was verified to exist in the pinned Buildroot 2024.02.9
# checkout before being added.

# Intel i915 GPU firmware — DMC (Display Microcontroller), GuC and HuC blobs.
#
# NOT optional on the newer FZ-55 generations, despite the older comment in
# kernel-fz55.fragment claiming i915 needs no firmware. That was true for the
# Mk1's UHD 620 (Whiskey Lake), where basic KMS works bare. It is NOT true for
# Mk2 (Iris Xe / Tiger Lake) or Mk3 (Iris Xe / Raptor Lake): those need DMC
# firmware for display power management, and without it i915 runs with display
# power states degraded or unavailable — which presents as flicker, failure to
# reach low-power display states, and measurably worse battery life on a
# vehicle terminal that is supposed to last a shift.
# Confirmed present in the pinned linux-firmware-20240115 tarball:
# i915/adlp_dmc*.bin (Alder Lake-P — the display IP Raptor Lake shares) and
# i915/tgl_dmc*.bin (Tiger Lake, Mk2).
BR2_PACKAGE_LINUX_FIRMWARE_I915=y

# Audio userspace. The kernel gives a sound device; something has to be able to
# set the mixer or every stream comes out muted at zero gain, which is
# indistinguishable from "audio does not work" to an officer.
# alsa-utils brings amixer/alsactl (used by the shell's volume control) and
# alsa-lib is what any application links against to open a PCM device at all.
BR2_PACKAGE_ALSA_UTILS=y
# alsa-utils installs NOTHING by default beyond a couple of tools — each
# command is its own sub-option. amixer specifically was absent from the built
# image despite BR2_PACKAGE_ALSA_UTILS=y, because it has to be asked for by
# name. amixer is the one the taskbar volume control actually shells out to;
# alsactl saves/restores mixer state across reboots (otherwise the terminal
# comes back muted every boot), and aplay is how any alert sound gets played.
BR2_PACKAGE_ALSA_UTILS_AMIXER=y
BR2_PACKAGE_ALSA_UTILS_ALSACTL=y
BR2_PACKAGE_ALSA_UTILS_APLAY=y
BR2_PACKAGE_ALSA_LIB=y

# Bluetooth userspace. bluez5_utils provides bluetoothd — without the daemon the
# kernel radio exists but nothing can pair, scan, or hold a connection, so a
# Bluetooth barcode scanner or headset is unusable even though the hardware is
# fully enabled.
BR2_PACKAGE_BLUEZ5_UTILS=y
